---
// The research section of shapelogic.xyz: papers and analysis from the solvers.
// The papers themselves are static pages in public/research/.
import '../../styles/landing.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Research | ShapeLogic</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Research and analysis about puzzles and shapes, and what the ShapeLogic solvers can tell us."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/puzzle_icon.jpg" />
    <style>
      .banner img.logo-bar { width: auto; max-width: 100%; }
      .post-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; column-gap: 16px; margin: 0 0 12px; }
      .post-head h3 { margin: 0; flex: 1 1 20em; }
      .post-date { color: var(--muted); font-size: 14px; white-space: nowrap; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="banner">
      <a href="/"><img class="logo-bar" src="/assets/shapelogic_logo.png" alt="ShapeLogic home" width="795" height="132" /></a>
    </header>

    <main>
      <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">ShapeLogic</a> › Research</nav>
      <h1>Research</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        This section is dedicated to research and analysis about puzzles and shapes, and what the
        ShapeLogic solvers can tell us about them. Each paper starts from real solver runs and
        reports what they show.
      </p>

      <section class="blog" aria-labelledby="solving-heading">
        <h2 class="blog-heading" id="solving-heading">Solving</h2>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="paper-breadth-first">
          <div class="post-head">
            <h3 id="paper-breadth-first">
              <a href="/research/repeated-positions.html">Puzzle Solving: Breadth First and Graphs</a>
            </h3>
            <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time>
          </div>
          <p>
            Why the moves of a puzzle make a graph and not a tree, what the take-apart search keeps
            of each position, what it does with the moves that lead back to positions it has
            already found, and what that saves on Pelikan Burr, a burr that needs 98 moves to open.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="paper-both-ends">
          <div class="post-head">
            <h3 id="paper-both-ends">
              <a href="/research/searching-from-both-ends.html">Puzzle Solving: Solving Faster with a Defined Solution</a>
            </h3>
            <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-11-16">16 November 2026</time>
          </div>
          <p>
            How the Sliding Puzzle Solver and the Panex Solver search from the start and from the
            goal at once: how the two ends find each other, which levels are dropped to save
            memory, and how the path is rebuilt once they meet.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="paper-optimized">
          <div class="post-head">
            <h3 id="paper-optimized">
              <a href="/research/optimized-paths.html">Puzzle Solving: Optimized Paths in Sliders</a>
            </h3>
            <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-11-16">16 November 2026</time>
          </div>
          <p>
            How the optimized paths option holds a sliding search to the grid its blocks fit, how
            far its move counts can be trusted as the fewest possible, and why the normal full
            search takes so much longer.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="paper-panex">
          <div class="post-head">
            <h3 id="paper-panex">
              <a href="/research/panex-solver-white-paper.html">Puzzle Solving: The Panex Stacking Puzzle</a>
            </h3>
            <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-10-30">30 October 2026</time>
          </div>
          <p>
            How ShapeLogic's Panex Solver finds shortest swaps, what eight solves (levels 3 to 10)
            show about the move counts, and where the solves could be made faster.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/research/panex-solver-white-paper.pdf" download>Download PDF</a>
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="blog" aria-labelledby="shapes-heading">
        <h2 class="blog-heading" id="shapes-heading">Shapes</h2>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="paper-golden">
          <div class="post-head">
            <h3 id="paper-golden">
              <a href="/research/golden-rhombus-puzzles.html">Golden Rhombus Puzzles</a>
            </h3>
            <time class="post-date" datetime="2026-10-30">30 October 2026</time>
          </div>
          <p>
            The angles behind the rhombic triacontahedron, the 30 rhombic pyramids that assemble
            into it, and the 30-stick weave that shows the pentagons and hexagons of a truncated
            icosahedron, with the maths needed to cut every piece.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <p><a href="/">&larr; Back to ShapeLogic</a></p>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <img src="/assets/puzzle_icon.jpg" alt="" width="40" height="40" />
      <p>
        ShapeLogic is free open-source software, built and maintained by Colin Deisenroth.<br />
        Contact: <a href="mailto:shapelogic.xyz@gmail.com">shapelogic.xyz@gmail.com</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
